    {
      "id": 259689,
      "name": "Jeysie",
      "username": "Jeysie",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jeysie/{size}/1868_2.png",
      "created_at": "2024-07-04T14:32:33.398Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"OddballPaladin\" data-post=\"4\" data-topic=\"30914\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/o/d26b3c/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> OddballPaladin:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was hoping to have something more like Superman vs the KKK rather than Captain America’s bicentennial special</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I can understand this, but I’d argue that metameta considerations factored in and considering the current mood of the country this is the story we needed even if it’s possibly slightly ahistorical from a comics history stance.</p>\n<p>I also agree with KateWalker that the Parsons bit was the perfect touch. I feel like we need more plots with unpowered extended Parsons family members in general, even if they’re just minor subplot ones.</p>\n<p>Especially in the older days, there’s gotta be lots of unpowered siblings out there to then have lots of cousins. Like, both my parents had five siblings, that was just what people did (heh) back then.</p>\n<p>In the letters section I loved the whole “which villain would save their nemesis” talk. Not only was it an actual interesting ethics question for once, but both the “Apostate would save Fanatic just to get to hold it over her forever” and the entire bit about Kismet being pettier than they want to admit were especially amazing.</p>",
      "post_number": 10,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2024-07-04T14:32:54.429Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": 4,
      "quote_count": 1,
      "incoming_link_count": 1,
      "reads": 18,
      "readers_count": 17,
      "score": 53.6,
      "yours": false,
      "topic_id": 30914,
      "topic_slug": "episode-287-of-the-letters-page-writers-room-legacy-independence-day-spectacular",
      "display_username": "Jeysie",
      "primary_group_name": null,
      "flair_name": null,
      "flair_url": null,
      "flair_bg_color": null,
      "flair_color": null,
      "flair_group_id": null,
      "badges_granted": [],
      "version": 1,
      "can_edit": false,
      "can_delete": false,
      "can_recover": false,
      "can_see_hidden_post": false,
      "can_wiki": false,
      "read": false,
      "user_title": null,
      "bookmarked": false,
      "raw": "[quote=\"OddballPaladin, post:4, topic:30914\"]\nI was hoping to have something more like Superman vs the KKK rather than Captain America’s bicentennial special\n[/quote]\nI can understand this, but I'd argue that metameta considerations factored in and considering the current mood of the country this is the story we needed even if it's possibly slightly ahistorical from a comics history stance.\n\nI also agree with KateWalker that the Parsons bit was the perfect touch. I feel like we need more plots with unpowered extended Parsons family members in general, even if they're just minor subplot ones.\n\nEspecially in the older days, there's gotta be lots of unpowered siblings out there to then have lots of cousins. Like, both my parents had five siblings, that was just what people did (heh) back then.\n\nIn the letters section I loved the whole \"which villain would save their nemesis\" talk. Not only was it an actual interesting ethics question for once, but both the \"Apostate would save Fanatic just to get to hold it over her forever\" and the entire bit about Kismet being pettier than they want to admit were especially amazing.",
